{
 "source_notes": "Data gathered 2026-10-15 via web-search snippets only (WebFetch blocked). Cycling Level = https://cyclinglevel.com/cycling-times/<d>-times (pages: 1k, 4k, 5k, 8k, 15k, 1 mile, 5 mile, 10k, 10 mile, 20k, 25k, 30k, 40k, 50k, 80k, 100k, 160k, 20 mile, 50 mile, 100 mile). Each page: male and female tables, ages 10-90 in 5-yr steps, 5 levels, plus companion tables of average speed (mph and/or km/h), power (watts), calories/hour and a 'points' grid. Site calls them 'average X cycle time by age and ability'. The site calculator 'will adapt your rating based on your age/gender/bodyweight', but no separate bodyweight tables were found. No statement of data source or sample size was found in any snippet (About page only says standards apply to different ages for a fairer comparison); unlike Strength Level, the user-submitted-data origin is NOT confirmed. The 'average for men/women' equals the age-25 intermediate cell; ages 20-30 are a plateau (identical for men). cyclingregimen.com (/blog/<d>-cycle-times) says its benchmarks are 'modelled estimates for flat solo efforts' using age-adjustment logic and pacing literature, not a race-result database, and its age tables are not sex-labelled. Column order everywhere: [Beginner, Novice, Intermediate, Advanced, Elite].",
 "levels_meaning": "Cycling Level (confirmed on 100 mile and 20k pages, same framing on 5 mile, 10 mile, 25k, 30k, 50k, 50 mile): Beginner = faster than 5% of cyclists; Novice = faster than 20%; Intermediate = faster than 50%; Advanced = faster than 80%; Elite = faster than 95%. Same as Strength Level. Also descriptive labels: beginner has cycled for at least a month; novice ~6 months regular riding; intermediate at least two years regular riding.",
